title: Spike app-server remote TUI and passive item injection
---
Origin: I001.

Implement a disposable, fixture-gated protocol probe for the installed Codex version. Launch `codex app-server --listen unix://PATH`, connect `codex --remote unix://PATH`, and use a second protocol client to resume the live thread and attempt `thread/inject_items`.

Acceptance criteria:
- Generate and commit no version-specific schema artifact; generate it under /tmp during the probe and record the Codex version plus relevant request/response shape in a research receipt.
- Prove whether injected items are accepted while the remote TUI is idle and while a turn is active.
- Verify that injection starts no new turn and does not use `turn/steer` or `turn/interrupt`.
- Treat all c2c message content as data; injection must not create an approval or execute an action.
- Establish the app-server control boundary before considering delivery: attempt unauthorized same-UID local-client connections and requests for `turn/start`, `turn/steer`, `turn/interrupt`, and other non-passive methods. A private Unix-path name or file mode alone is not evidence of isolation.
- Record the exact boundary (for example an authenticated transport plus protected secret, or an OS/container identity boundary), its threat model, and its failure behavior. If no boundary prevents another swarm process from controlling the server, return a no-go for this architecture rather than proceeding.
- Record exact failure modes and a go/no-go decision for implementation tasks.

Blockers: none; this is the evidence-producing prerequisite, including the control-boundary go/no-go.
